* START-HISTORY:
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - RDPACCOUNT and NO.RDPACCOUNT are gone; SSH, NO.SSH,
*   API and NO.API replace them.  Owner's decision, 21 Aug 2026: Remote Desktop
*   is out of this port's focus, and the rule it obstructed - nobody SD creates
*   reaches the keyboard unless they are an administrator - now holds by
*   construction.  See CREATEA's sdsshonly branch.
* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - RDPACCOUNT and NO.RDPACCOUNT move an account in and
*   out of sdsshonly, which is what decides whether it may sign in to Windows.
*   PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8.  Nothing is recorded in ACCOUNTS: the group
*   membership IS the property.
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - SDSYS accounts is lower case on disk.  5.12 (a).
* rev 0.9.0 Jan 25 mab initial release
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account <ADD/DELETE> user.name
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SSH               - may sign in over ssh
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account NO.SSH            - may not sign in over ssh
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account API               - may use the API
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account NO.API            - may not use the API
*
* THE TWO REMOTE ROUTES, AND THERE IS NO THIRD.  The console and Remote Desktop
* are denied to every account SD creates - sdsshonly, applied by CREATE.ACCOUNT
* and enforced by gplbld/deny-logon.ps1 - and no verb here lifts that.  An
* administrator reaches the keyboard because Windows already calls them one,
* not because SD granted it.
*
* EACH IS ONE GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING IS WRITTEN TO ACCOUNTS.  sdssh is
* what sshd's AllowGroups names; sdapi is what APISRVR tests after the SCRAM
* proof succeeds.  A mirrored field could only ever disagree with the thing
* that actually decides - the reasoning that deleted ACC$USERS on 15 Aug.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $modify.account
$catalog $MODIFYA

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h
* 
deffun is_grp_member(user,group) calling '!is_grp_member'
deffun os_group(action,group,member) calling '!os_group'
deffun is_group(group) calling '!is_group'
deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

   prompt ''

   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end     

   parser = "!PARSER"

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS     ;* Preset for argument errors
   
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      @system.return.code = -status()
      stop sysmsg(6000) ;* Cannot open ACCOUNTS file in SDSYS account
   end

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword) ;* Verb modify.Account

   * Get next token, account name
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      crt 'Command Syntax:  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> ADD | DELETE <username>'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH | NO.SSH'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API | NO.API'
      stop
   end
   *
   acc.name = upcase(acc.name)   ;* account names are always upper case
   if acc.name = 'SDSYS' then
      stop sysmsg(2202) ;* Account name is invalid
   end   
   *
   read acc.rec from acc.f, acc.name else      
      stop sysmsg(6003) ;* Account name is invalid
   end
   
   group.name = acc.rec<ACC$GROUP>   ;* get associated group
   
   if is_group(group.name) then
      * Get next token, ADD or DELETE
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, action, keyword)

* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - SSH, NO.SSH, API and NO.API, replacing RDPACCOUNT
*   and NO.RDPACCOUNT.  Four more actions in the slot ADD and DELETE occupy:
*
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH      may sign in over ssh
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NO.SSH   may not
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API      may use the API
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NO.API   may not
*
*   They take NO user name - they act on the account's OWN Windows user - so
*   they are handled before the ADD/DELETE test rather than inside it, which
*   would demand one.
*
*   THE PROPERTY IS GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING ELSE, which is why all four
*   share one pair of routines with the group name and the four messages passed
*   in.  Nothing is written to ACCOUNTS - owner's decision, 20 Aug 2026, kept -
*   so there is no record that could disagree with the group.
*
*   WRAPPED ROUND THE ADD/DELETE BLOCK RATHER THAN FOLDED INTO IT.  That block
*   is working code with four message paths in it; turning its "if ADD or
*   DELETE" into arms of a case statement would rewrite all of it to add
*   actions that share none of it.  One "if not(route.done)" leaves it
*   untouched.
      route.done = @true
      begin case
         case upcase(action) = 'SSH'
            route.group = 'sdssh'
            msg.done = 10063  ;* %1 may now sign in over ssh
            msg.already = 10064  ;* %1 may already sign in over ssh
            msg.fail = 10067  ;* Unable to change ssh access for %1, status: %2
            gosub route.add
         case upcase(action) = 'NO.SSH'
            route.group = 'sdssh'
            msg.done = 10065  ;* %1 can no longer sign in over ssh
            msg.already = 10066  ;* %1 already cannot sign in over ssh
            msg.fail = 10067  ;* Unable to change ssh access for %1, status: %2
            gosub route.remove
         case upcase(action) = 'API'
            route.group = 'sdapi'
            msg.done = 10068  ;* %1 may now use the API
            msg.already = 10069  ;* %1 may already use the API
            msg.fail = 10072  ;* Unable to change API access for %1, status: %2
            gosub route.add
         case upcase(action) = 'NO.API'
            route.group = 'sdapi'
            msg.done = 10070  ;* %1 can no longer use the API
            msg.already = 10071  ;* %1 already cannot use the API
            msg.fail = 10072  ;* Unable to change API access for %1, status: %2
            gosub route.remove
         case 1
            route.done = @false
      end case

      if not(route.done) then
      if (upcase(action) = 'ADD') or (upcase(action) = 'DELETE') then
      *  valid action
      *   Get next token, user name
         call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, user.name, keyword) 
         if token.type = PARSER$END then
            stop sysmsg(10016)  ;* Missing User Name
         end
         
         if (upcase(action) = 'ADD') then
            * Add user to group        
            * does user belong to sdusers?
            if is_grp_member(user.name,'sdusers') then
            * this user looks legit, add to group
            * first check to see if user is already a member of the group
               if is_grp_member(user.name,group.name) THEN
                  crt sysmsg(10017,user.name,group.name)  ;* %1 is already a member of group %2
               end else
                  * Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
                  * Use shared !valid_os_name before os.execute (phase 2).
                  * if convert(os.name.chars, '', user.name) # '' ...
                  if not(valid_os_name(user.name)) or not(valid_os_name(group.name)) then
                     crt sysmsg(10019,user.name,group.name,2) ;* Unable to add user %1 to group %s status: %2
                  end else
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: os.execute "sudo usermod -aG ..."
                  stat = os_group("ADDMEM", group.name, user.name)
                  * --------------------
                  if stat = 0 then
                     crt sysmsg(10018,user.name,group.name)  ;* %1 added to group %2
                  end else
                     crt sysmsg(10019,user.name,group.name,stat) ;* Unable to add user %1 to group %s status: %2
                  end
                  end
               end 
            end else
               crt user.name
               crt sysmsg(10020,user.name) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
            end 
         
         end else
            * already tested action, must be delete
            if is_grp_member(user.name,group.name) THEN
               * Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
               * Use shared !valid_os_name before os.execute (phase 2).
               if not(valid_os_name(user.name)) or not(valid_os_name(group.name)) then
                  crt sysmsg(10022,user.name,group.name,2) ;* Unable to remove user %1 from group %s status: %2
               end else
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: os.execute "sudo deluser ..."
               stat = os_group("DELMEM", group.name, user.name)
               * --------------------
                  if stat = 0 then
                     crt sysmsg(10021,user.name,group.name) ;*  %1 removed from group %2
                  end else
                     crt sysmsg(10022,user.name,group.name,stat) ;* Unable to remove user %1 from group %s status: %2
                  end
               end
            end else
               crt sysmsg(10023,user.name,group.name) ;* %1 is not a member of  %2
            end 
          
         end
         
         
      end else
         crt 'MODIFY.ACCOUNT Action Must Be ADD, DELETE, SSH, NO.SSH, API or NO.API'
      end
      end       ;* not(route.done)

   end else
     crt sysmsg(10024,group.name) ;* %1 is not a valid Windows group
   end
   goto program.end

* ======================================================================
* The account's OWN Windows user.
*
* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - DERIVED FROM ACC$GROUP, NOT FROM THE RECORD ID.
*   CREATE.ACCOUNT downcases the user name (CREATEA) and writes the ACCOUNTS
*   record under the UPPER case name, so downcase(acc.name) would usually be
*   right - but "usually" is doing real work there and this decides whether
*   somebody can log in.  ACC$GROUP is "sdu_":acc.uname, written by
*   CREATE.ACCOUNT from the user name itself, so taking the name back out of it
*   is a reversal of what was actually recorded rather than a guess at it.
*
*   AND THE RESULT IS CHECKED AGAINST sdusers before anything acts on it, with
*   the same test the ADD path above already uses.  That is what rejects a
*   GROUP-type account: its ACC$GROUP is a name the caller supplied, not
*   "sdu_"-anything, so the name that comes out is not an SD user and the
*   check refuses it.  A GROUP account has no single Windows user for this to
*   mean anything about.
account.user:
   acc.user = ''
   if group.name[1,4] = 'sdu_' then acc.user = group.name[5,999]
   if acc.user = '' then
      crt sysmsg(10020,acc.name) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
      user.ok = @false
      return
   end
   user.ok = is_grp_member(acc.user,'sdusers')
   if not(user.ok) then
      crt sysmsg(10020,acc.user) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* SSH / API - let this account use a remote route, by putting its own Windows
* user into the group that route is allowed by.
*
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - ONE PAIR OF ROUTINES FOR BOTH ROUTES.  The caller
*   sets route.group and the three message numbers; nothing else differs
*   between "may ssh" and "may use the API", and two copies of this would be
*   two places for the next fix to miss.
*
*   IDEMPOTENT, AND IT SAYS SO.  os_group ADDMEM succeeds on a member that is
*   already there, so asking first is what lets this report "already" rather
*   than implying it did something - the rule GRANTA states at its own ADDMEM.
*
*   NO ADMINISTRATOR GUARD HERE, UNLIKE THE ROUTINE THIS REPLACED.  That guard
*   existed because NO.RDPACCOUNT wrote to sdsshonly, which carries
*   SeDenyInteractiveLogonRight - so running it on an administrator's own
*   account locked them out of their console, which is what happened on 15 Aug
*   2026 the first time ADOPT ran.  NEITHER GROUP HERE CAN DO THAT.  sdsshonly
*   is not touched by any verb in this program any more, and sdssh cannot lock
*   an administrator out of ssh because allow-ssh-groups.ps1 names the
*   Administrators group in AllowGroups in its own right.  If a future route
*   ever writes to sdsshonly again, the guard comes back with it.
route.add:
   gosub account.user
   if not(user.ok) then return

   if is_grp_member(acc.user, route.group) then
      crt sysmsg(msg.already, acc.user)
      return
   end

   if not(valid_os_name(acc.user)) then
      crt sysmsg(msg.fail, acc.user, 2)
      return
   end

   stat = os_group("ADDMEM", route.group, acc.user)
   if stat = 0 then
      crt sysmsg(msg.done, acc.user)
   end else
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(msg.fail, acc.user, stat)
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* NO.SSH / NO.API - withdraw a remote route, by taking the account's own
* Windows user back out of that route's group.
*
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - THE WITHDRAWAL IS IMMEDIATE FOR BOTH ROUTES, and
*   that is worth knowing because the grant is not.  sshd re-reads its
*   AllowGroups per connection and APISRVR asks the SAM per login, so neither
*   consults a token; but a Windows access token fixes group membership at
*   sign-in, so an account GRANTED a route mid-session may still need to sign
*   out and back in before anything token-based agrees.  Nothing here depends
*   on a token, so NO.SSH and NO.API bite at once.
route.remove:
   gosub account.user
   if not(user.ok) then return

   if not(is_grp_member(acc.user, route.group)) then
      crt sysmsg(msg.already, acc.user)
      return
   end

   if not(valid_os_name(acc.user)) then
      crt sysmsg(msg.fail, acc.user, 2)
      return
   end

   stat = os_group("DELMEM", route.group, acc.user)
   if stat = 0 then
      crt sysmsg(msg.done, acc.user)
   end else
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(msg.fail, acc.user, stat)
   end
   return

program.end:
   END
